00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:22.990
Now let’s go back to our linear algebra.

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:28.140
In this paper, the authors say “we consider
problems of linear algebra…”

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:29.140
"We"?

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:31.110
Who now?

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.710
We, François and Charton, right?

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:38.150
Just kidding, we are laughing about the scientific
way of writing “we”, even when the paper

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:40.260
was written by a single author.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.920
There are even better things to laugh about
when it comes to scientific publishing, like

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.960
for example the case of the physicist who
used his cat [cat sound] as a co-author to

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.710
conform to publication standards.

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:55.530
Read that story on Wikipedia, it’s linked
in the description below.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:03:03.400
So, they, the authors, use a transformer to
perform 9 numerical calculations from linear

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:09.030
algebra:
Matrix transposition, matrix addition, matrix-vector

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:16.070
multiplications, eigenvalues and eigenvectors,
singular values and SVD and finally, matrix

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:17.290
inversion.

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:21.020
But why would anyone ever want to do that!?

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:25.290
It’s not like we do not have algorithms
for these problems already!

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.820
Why do matrix inversion with transformers?

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:32.820
Well, we have at least one reason we could
present to you: I think Ms. Coffee Bean is

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:37.850
not the only one who is curious to know:
What are the limits of what transformers can

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:38.850
do?

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:40.690
Can transformers be applied on text?

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:42.710
Oh yes, they can!

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:44.020
Can they process images?

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:45.060
Yes, they can!

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.120
Do they work on speech?

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:48.630
Of course, they do.

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:49.920
Can they do math?

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:52.910
Well, it depends on what kind of math.

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:57.900
We had a video where we explained the transformers
that do symbolic integration.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:03.480
Transformers also have been shown to prove
theorems, do formal logic, solve SAT problems,

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:08.480
symbolic regression, dynamical systems, and
so on, see the paper for references.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:12.930
So, for symbolic mathematics, transformers
seem to work well.

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:14.290
But how are they with numbers?

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:18.220
Luckily, this is what the authors are basically
asking too.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:23.889
There have been investigations on sequence-to-sequence
models for arithmetic tasks, showing how transformers

00:04:23.889 --> 00:04:25.939
do not work that well on this.

00:04:25.939 --> 00:04:29.580
Check out the related work if you are interested
to know about this part.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:36.150
But the linear algebra problems as chosen
by the author(s) answer a broader spectrum

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:40.509
of questions, from little easy tasks to complex
ones:

00:04:40.509 --> 00:04:45.750
Can transformers do basic operations on individual
coefficients of matrices, like it is the case

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:48.759
with matrix transposition and addition?

00:04:48.759 --> 00:04:54.320
Can they also do compositions of several arithmetic
operations, like it is the case with matrix

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:55.419
multiplication?

00:04:55.419 --> 00:05:01.800
And then, can they even do complex nonlinear
transformations (with cubic complexity) like

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:05.669
the matrix decompositions and matrix inversion?

00:05:05.669 --> 00:05:09.470
And then: What about generalization?

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:16.479
Does a transformer trained on one type of
matrices generalize to another type of matrices?

00:05:16.479 --> 00:05:19.699
And also, what about few shot learning?

00:05:19.699 --> 00:05:25.910
Can trained transformers be further tuned
to solve even larger problems by only a few

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:26.910
examples?

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:30.259
A few, this is few-shot learning, after all.

00:05:30.259 --> 00:05:31.259
So you see?

00:05:31.259 --> 00:05:36.930
The test here is big, ranging from simple
operations to more complicated arithmetic,

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:43.150
to advanced algorithms I learned about in
university, in my linear algebra classes.

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:46.440
Ms. Coffee Bean, to date has not heard of
them yet,

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:52.499
so she calls NumPy linalg all the time in
her blissful programmer ignorance.

00:05:52.499 --> 00:05:59.199
But I see you are not convinced or motivated
yet to use the transformer overkill to solve

00:05:59.199 --> 00:06:00.909
these problems?

00:06:00.909 --> 00:06:02.629
Are you reviewer 2 here?

00:06:02.629 --> 00:06:08.930
Then let’s look beyond the paper into even
more arguments made by the author(s) during

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:10.400
their rebuttal.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:15.559
One argument is that is important to test
the limits of transformers if we want to apply

00:06:15.559 --> 00:06:22.120
these to science, just think about Deepmind’s
AlphaFold in biology or transformers as a

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:23.659
replacement for Mathematica.

00:06:23.659 --> 00:06:30.610
So the question is whether certain computational
building blocks can be performed inside a

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:31.610
transformer.

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:37.610
Because if yes, then one step is clear when
chasing the question of applicability of transformers

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:40.229
in science: they can do linear algebra.

00:06:40.229 --> 00:06:47.169
One more argument that the author(s) make,
which Ms. Coffee Bean seems to like the most,

00:06:47.169 --> 00:06:51.259
is that transformers are usually used as end-to-end
solutions.

00:06:51.259 --> 00:06:57.289
Let’s suppose that for the solution, we
must compute some arithmetic operation or

00:06:57.289 --> 00:06:59.129
some linear algebra.

00:06:59.129 --> 00:07:05.369
It would be impractical (when time and resources
are critical) to “step out” of the transformer

00:07:05.369 --> 00:07:12.029
to perform these operations, so having a network,
or a subnetwork implementing these operations

00:07:12.029 --> 00:07:15.849
in neurons, is simply: comfortable and practical.

00:07:15.849 --> 00:07:22.689
Two more arguments focus on providing general
insights on transformers that are small.

00:07:22.689 --> 00:07:27.909
We are not talking about GPT-3 sized models
here, but about shallow, simple models that

00:07:27.909 --> 00:07:31.279
one scientist can train on his GPU at home.

00:07:31.279 --> 00:07:36.100
Also, the encodings, representations and insight
can be used for purposes other than linear

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:37.100
algebra.

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:39.879
Okay, Ms. Coffee Bean, enough with the motivation.

00:07:39.879 --> 00:07:41.900
We got it; this is research.

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:45.719
It has all the positive practical outcomes
we just mentioned.

00:07:45.719 --> 00:07:50.639
It’s fine to do something only because we
can and because we can learn something from

00:07:50.639 --> 00:07:51.639
doing it.

00:07:51.639 --> 00:07:56.379
It is research, it does not have to land directly
into the AppStore to make money out of it.

00:07:56.379 --> 00:08:02.289
In any case, now that we are over-motivated
with transformers solving linear algebra problems

00:08:02.289 --> 00:08:07.449
and we think that it is cool, let’s dive
into explaining how this is done.

00:08:07.449 --> 00:08:14.819
Well, to work on matrices with transformers,
one needs two things: matrices and transformers.

00:08:14.819 --> 00:08:20.349
Of course, the challenge here is to represent
the matrices, so the input, into a format

00:08:20.349 --> 00:08:22.250
that a transformer can process.

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:26.599
The other challenge is to understand transformers,
for this, maybe you want to check out one

00:08:26.599 --> 00:08:28.729
of our previous videos.

00:08:28.729 --> 00:08:34.419
We remember that transformers work with sets
and, if the set's elements have an order,

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:36.560
we speak of sequential data.

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:40.860
The transformer encodes the order of the data
in positional embeddings.

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:47.170
And matrices have a structure, an order so
we need to somehow transform these two-dimensional

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:49.840
tables into sequences.

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:54.290
Nothing easier than this, one can do it by
taking the numbers and writing them one after

00:08:54.290 --> 00:08:55.290
the other.

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:58.270
Their order would be then captured in positional
embeddings.

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:03.770
The author(s) also pre-pend a coding of the
size of the matrix.

00:09:03.770 --> 00:09:08.531
This gives the transformer further information
about the matrix dimensionality so the transformer

00:09:08.531 --> 00:09:10.710
can leverage this.

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:13.180
But what have we done, Ms. Coffee Bean?

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:18.900
By just writing the numbers one after the
other, we actually created a vocabulary for

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:25.290
the transformer of infinite size, because
there are infinitely many real numbers (the

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:26.790
last we checked).

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:33.020
So encoding each matrix coefficient with just
the number it represents, is a poor decision

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:38.740
because the network would need to learn representations
for the each possible number in the infinitely

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:41.400
sized vocabulary.

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:45.800
With an infinite vocabulary size, it will
surely happen that numbers occurring during

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:49.070
testing, will not have occurred during training.

00:09:49.070 --> 00:09:54.020
Then is there a way to encode the numbers
better and maybe have them represent some

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:56.670
prior knowledge about numbers in general?

00:09:56.670 --> 00:10:00.660
Yes, there are at least 4, as presented by
the author(s).

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:05.890
We are talking about P10, P1000, B1999, ... and
... .

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:10.500
Ms. Coffee Bean, stop it with the cryptic
codes, we are not talking about the Terminator.

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:15.340
So what is meant, is pretty clear from this
table, that one can decompose a floating point

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:20.980
number according to these rules and use a
set of integer numbers (still infinite, but

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:24.720
their infinity is smaller than the infinity
of the real numbers) and other symbols.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:28.240
This means that the knowledge about numbers
is explicated.

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:35.010
For example, in the P10 and P1000 representations,
we explicitly have the information of whether

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:41.460
the number is positive or negative, and this
is explicitly stated in a separate token.

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:46.470
So yes, each number is tokenized into 5 tokens
in the P10 representation, so the sequence

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:52.830
length is blown up by 5 times, but this also
means that the sign of the number is a special

00:10:52.830 --> 00:10:58.740
token in the vocabulary, so the plus token
is shared among all positive numbers in the

00:10:58.740 --> 00:10:59.780
vocabulary.

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:05.000
If we get a new, yet unseen number during
testing, with the P10 representation, the

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:11.110
transformer will have an easier time: the
sign token is known, these integer tokens

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:12.740
are also known.

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:17.270
So there is a trade-off between how much a
number is decomposed into a known vocabulary

00:11:17.270 --> 00:11:23.350
(making things easier) and the sequence length
(making things also harder when training transformers).

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:28.410
Cool, so now that we know how the authors
encode their matrices into sequences, they

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:30.760
just need to create training data.

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:31.760
How?

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:35.150
Is there a matrix database or benchmark lying
around anywhere?

00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:40.510
No need, because one can generate random matrices
oneself for the input.

00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:46.360
To generate the output, one just has to call
NumPy linalg and get the solution.

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:51.970
So say welcome to matrix-solution pairs, gather
your transformer and let them do their thing.

00:11:51.970 --> 00:12:00.030
As a note, the paper does work with relatively
small matrices , like 5x5, 6x4, 2x13.

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:04.640
Of course, a next research question is postponed
for the future, about bigger matrices, thus

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:07.020
longer sequences for the transformer.

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:13.710
In any case, training processes later, the
author(s) come in to check onto their transformers

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:16.610
and how well they did on each task.

00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:21.290
And well, surprise, 90+% accuracy across the
board!

00:12:21.290 --> 00:12:25.890
At least within certain tolerance levels (neural
networks are probabilistic models, so one

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:29.440
cannot expect exact results in each matrix
coefficient).

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:34.990
Yes, there are some differences between encodings
where P1000 generally performs best.

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:39.690
But the short lesson here, is:
transformers do work on these 9 problems!

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:43.300
Feel free to gaze at the numbers in the paper
linked below.

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:48.140
Because the “real” question about neural
networks is: All-right, but how does this

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:49.140
generalize?

00:12:49.140 --> 00:12:54.130
(This is AI researcher talk for asking whether
the model has really “understanding” the

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:55.130
task.

00:12:55.130 --> 00:12:58.790
Ms. Coffee Bean, why are you using the word
“understanding”, that’s off limits.

00:12:58.790 --> 00:13:04.750
Let’s reformulate: so the question is whether
the transformer has found the general algorithm

00:13:04.750 --> 00:13:10.530
behind, let’s say matrix transposition,
so can it generalize to types of matrices

00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:14.470
it has not seen during training -- that would
be generalization.

00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:21.590
And this is what the author(s) investigate
in two ways:

00:13:21.590 --> 00:13:24.700
The out-of-distribution generalization experiment.

00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:29.760
In a nutshell: the authors train their models
with random n by n matrices that have the

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:34.760
property that the coefficients are independent
and identically distributed (iid) sampled

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:37.760
from a uniform distribution.

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:40.880
Matrices fulfilling this property are known
as Wigner matrices.

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:44.380
Okay, so we have the Wigner matrices.

00:13:44.380 --> 00:13:50.630
How does a transformer trained on Wigner matrices
perform on other types of matrices (also present

00:13:50.630 --> 00:13:51.860
in science)?

00:13:51.860 --> 00:13:57.700
Well, if the test set is composed of matrices
with coefficients that just have a different

00:13:57.700 --> 00:14:03.930
standard deviation than the train set, the
model only generalizes if the variance of

00:14:03.930 --> 00:14:09.480
the coefficients in the test set is lower
than the training set variance.

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:13.550
If one increases the variance, the probability
to meet a number outside the training range

00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:18.070
increases too and the model kind of fails
because it cannot extrapolate.

00:14:18.070 --> 00:14:21.860
Ms. Coffee Bean counts this as a “nay”
for generalization.

00:14:21.860 --> 00:14:27.050
We think Gary Marcus would say: Told you so,
neural nets do not extrapolate!

00:14:27.050 --> 00:14:32.820
Okay, then let’s forget about the coefficient
variance and let’s look at the experiments

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:36.660
where the eigenvalues of the matrices have
different distributions.

00:14:36.660 --> 00:14:42.530
Well, again, if the distribution changes from
train to test set, the results are again bad

00:14:42.530 --> 00:14:45.370
if not catastrophic for some configurations.

00:14:45.370 --> 00:14:49.490
Then what to do in order to generalize?

00:14:49.490 --> 00:14:53.320
Spoiler: (carefully) adjust the training data.

00:14:53.320 --> 00:14:57.490
What helps generalization is when -- hold
on to your chair --

00:14:57.490 --> 00:15:02.800
the training set is enlarged such that its
distribution captures more of the different

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:04.660
types of matrices.

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:09.070
This goes exactly towards the point we made
in our video about foundation models that

00:15:09.070 --> 00:15:14.680
of course “out-of-distribution” generalization
happens when we increased the training distribution

00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:17.560
so much that the whole world was in it!

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:22.230
Yes, this paper is not enough to draw this
conclusion, but hints into this direction.

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:28.430
So no, we do not agree with the points made
by the authors here that actual generalization

00:15:28.430 --> 00:15:29.430
happens.

00:15:29.430 --> 00:15:34.640
It might very well be that we mean something
different with “generalization”, we think.

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:40.410
But the point of the authors maybe is that
by varying the training set, one can increase

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:43.250
performance on out-of-domain tasks.

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:48.190
But Ms. Coffee Bean is allergic to these big
words and argues that this is not the point

00:15:48.190 --> 00:15:52.750
of generalization, because generalization
happens when the model “got it” and found

00:15:52.750 --> 00:15:59.000
the algorithm behind eigenvalue decomposition
and can apply it successfully to really unseen

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:00.000
distributions.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.150
But yes, it is too much to expect, sure.

00:16:02.150 --> 00:16:09.010
And if we are already at the generalization
buzzword, let’s go to the next buzzword:

00:16:09.010 --> 00:16:10.010
Few-shot.

00:16:10.010 --> 00:16:15.960
The argument of the authors here is that the
models trained on small matrices (5 x 5) do

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:18.130
not generalize to larger matrices.

00:16:18.130 --> 00:16:23.130
Then the model trained on 40 million examples
can be further trained to also solve bigger

00:16:23.130 --> 00:16:27.350
matrices with about 25 million more examples.

00:16:27.350 --> 00:16:28.350
Ok.

00:16:28.350 --> 00:16:29.770
Sounds fair.

00:16:29.770 --> 00:16:34.920
But what does not sound fair is that this
is put into relation with the word few-shot!

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:38.730
Is 25M examples few-shot now!?

00:16:38.730 --> 00:16:44.310
Is it just Ms. Coffee Bean for whom few-shot
is at most 20 or okay, 100 examples?

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:48.380
Since when is few-shot in the order of millions
and more than half the amount of training

00:16:48.380 --> 00:16:51.020
data during initial training?

00:16:51.020 --> 00:16:52.020
What do you think?

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:54.029
What is “few” in few-shot?

00:16:54.029 --> 00:16:58.740
And is this successful out-of-domain generalization
as presented in the paper, or just wishful

00:16:58.740 --> 00:17:00.340
thinking but not quite?

00:17:00.340 --> 00:17:05.620
Or let’s make “generalization” less
of a binary variable, so we want to ask you:

00:17:05.620 --> 00:17:08.000
how much percent generalization is here?

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:10.600
Let us know what you think in the comments.

00:17:10.600 --> 00:17:15.040
Ok, Ms. Coffee Bean, the terminology police-coffee-bean
will go now.

00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:18.570
Do not take her too serious on her rants about
buzzwords.

00:17:18.570 --> 00:17:23.860
I am writing papers myself and I must admit
that buzzwords… help!

00:17:23.860 --> 00:17:30.380
If these are loosely defined, evolving, and
changing overused words, why not use them

00:17:30.380 --> 00:17:32.220
to one’s own advantage?

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:34.309
Hey, Ms. Coffee Bean, you still here?

00:17:34.309 --> 00:17:37.080
Yes you are, because you have something to
tell:

00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:42.100
Look, we have been doing this AI Coffee Break
Quiz on our community tab for quite a while

00:17:42.100 --> 00:17:43.100
now.

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:45.360
We have posted over 200 questions there already.

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:48.800
And yes, we are kind of running out of ideas.

00:17:48.800 --> 00:17:56.039
So if you, dear audience member, want to contribute
over there and you have an idea for a question,

00:17:56.039 --> 00:18:00.040
just email us and we will post it [the suggestion]
and we will also credit you.

00:18:00.040 --> 00:18:00.150
Okay, bye!

